WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:01.042
This is totally backwards

00:00:01.042 --> 00:00:02.502
from how you're supposed
to do a startup, right?

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:03.878
You're supposed to have a problem

00:00:03.878 --> 00:00:05.714
and we had no idea what the problem was.

00:00:05.714 --> 00:00:07.799
Is there a world where
the AI becomes the manager

00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:09.050
and it, you know, gives you ideas

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:10.468
and gives you some tasks to do?

00:00:10.468 --> 00:00:12.554
This was probably the hardest
project that I've ever done,

00:00:12.554 --> 00:00:13.805
because it felt totally doomed, right?

00:00:13.805 --> 00:00:16.057
It's like, I know like every instinct,

00:00:16.057 --> 00:00:17.559
every builder instinct of mine.

00:00:17.559 --> 00:00:18.393
Did it actually feel doomed?

00:00:18.393 --> 00:00:19.477
Oh, I felt totally doomed.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.605
Greg in 2010 dropped out of MIT

00:00:22.605 --> 00:00:24.315
to become our first engineer

00:00:24.315 --> 00:00:26.609
and went on to become Stripe's CTO.

00:00:26.609 --> 00:00:29.612
In 2015 after he left,
he co-founded OpenAI.

00:00:29.612 --> 00:00:31.906
OpenAI is really cooking at the moment

00:00:31.906 --> 00:00:33.908
and Greg is one of the most
productive people I know.

00:00:33.908 --> 00:00:34.743
Cheers.

00:00:37.746 --> 00:00:40.540
Wow, you really have
some old school photos.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:42.834
Some deep cut GDB trivia.

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:45.628
So yeah. We figure we've
gotta put people at ease,

00:00:45.628 --> 00:00:47.005
make people feel at home.

00:00:47.005 --> 00:00:48.256
Very nice. Thank you. Okay.

00:00:48.256 --> 00:00:50.592
Well, I just dive straight
into all the questions I have,

00:00:50.592 --> 00:00:51.676
which is a lot.
All right.

00:00:51.676 --> 00:00:53.595
If you were not working in AI,

00:00:53.595 --> 00:00:55.263
how could one have known that

00:00:55.263 --> 00:00:56.723
something was about to start working?

00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:58.600
People were telling you
that AI was the future

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.352
in the 1970s, in the 1980s, the 1990s.

00:01:01.352 --> 00:01:02.979
Then very quickly, in the late 2010s

00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:04.105
everything started happening.

00:01:04.105 --> 00:01:07.067
Well, I was someone who
was not in the field

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:09.652
and so, I remember very
much what it was like.

00:01:09.652 --> 00:01:13.448
2013, 2014, it felt like
every day on Hacker News

00:01:13.448 --> 00:01:16.701
there'd be a new "deep
learning for X" article.

00:01:16.701 --> 00:01:18.953
And I remember being like,
"What is deep learning?

00:01:18.953 --> 00:01:21.956
I knew, like, one person in the field,

00:01:21.956 --> 00:01:24.250
and I asked them to introduce me

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:25.543
to more people in the field,

00:01:25.543 --> 00:01:27.253
and I just kept getting introduced

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.130
to a bunch of my smartest
friends from college.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:32.175
Now, if you actually look at
the work that was being done...

00:01:32.175 --> 00:01:34.177
2012, basically,

00:01:34.177 --> 00:01:35.637
image recognition, for the first time,

00:01:35.637 --> 00:01:36.805
you could solve with the neural net

00:01:36.805 --> 00:01:38.306
much better than anything else.

00:01:38.306 --> 00:01:39.891
It just blew all these

00:01:39.891 --> 00:01:41.935
traditional computer vision
approaches out of the water.

00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:43.478
It's like this like learned system

00:01:43.478 --> 00:01:45.563
is able to outperform 40
years worth of, "Let's

00:01:45.563 --> 00:01:47.023
write down all the rules and try to

00:01:47.023 --> 00:01:49.901
handcraft the algorithm for the task."

00:01:49.901 --> 00:01:52.529
And it's very easy to then be like,

00:01:52.529 --> 00:01:54.155
"Okay. Well, this approach,

00:01:54.155 --> 00:01:55.281
sure it works for computer vision,

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:57.826
but it's never gonna work
for machine translation."

00:01:57.826 --> 00:01:58.993
In 2014,

00:01:58.993 --> 00:02:00.203
suddenly, you're getting great results

00:02:00.203 --> 00:02:01.329
in machine translation.

00:02:01.329 --> 00:02:03.498
I think that this pattern was applied

00:02:03.498 --> 00:02:05.500
in subfield after subfield.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.544
One thing I've been wondering about is

00:02:07.544 --> 00:02:08.795
so many different things

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:12.006
are finally working at the same time.

00:02:12.006 --> 00:02:14.717
We have LLMs, which are obviously amazing,

00:02:14.717 --> 00:02:16.803
but then we also separately have

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:18.096
image models really working.

00:02:18.096 --> 00:02:20.557
We also have text-to-speech
and speech-to-text

00:02:20.557 --> 00:02:21.933
working way better than they were before.

00:02:21.933 --> 00:02:24.185
What's the common factor

00:02:24.185 --> 00:02:26.896
behind everything starting
to work at the same time?

00:02:26.896 --> 00:02:28.439
Well, it's deep learning, right?

00:02:28.439 --> 00:02:29.774
I think deep learning is the core-

00:02:29.774 --> 00:02:30.608
We've have deep learning for a long time.

00:02:30.608 --> 00:02:32.360
Why didn't deep learning
work in the 1980s?

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.445
If you look at the number of orders

00:02:34.445 --> 00:02:35.488
of magnitude of compute that

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:38.533
we've gone through from 1940 to today,

00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:40.702
I mean, it's just astounding.

00:02:40.702 --> 00:02:43.329
You think they're all
explained by compute scale-ups

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:44.414
applied to the right algorithms?

00:02:44.414 --> 00:02:45.790
Of course, the type of algorithm changes,

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.292
and some of those results
aren't even deep learning.

00:02:47.292 --> 00:02:49.210
But I think that fundamentally,
it is about compute,

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:51.004
and you need an algorithm that is scalable

00:02:51.004 --> 00:02:52.505
that can actually absorb that compute.

00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:53.631
Was OpenAI the first company

00:02:53.631 --> 00:02:57.677
to take the scaling
hypothesis really seriously?

00:02:57.677 --> 00:03:00.138
I think that claiming the
first is always difficult,

00:03:00.138 --> 00:03:02.473
but I think that is clear that we sort of

00:03:02.473 --> 00:03:06.102
succeeded much more wildly
sooner than anyone else.

00:03:06.102 --> 00:03:07.896
I think that we had real conviction

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:09.814
behind what we needed to do.

00:03:09.814 --> 00:03:11.524
Some people think that OpenAI

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:13.693
set out to prove the scale hypothesis,

00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:15.486
whereas it was almost
the other way around,

00:03:15.486 --> 00:03:17.322
that the scale hypothesis
is what we observed

00:03:17.322 --> 00:03:19.657
as the thing that was working for us,

00:03:19.657 --> 00:03:21.951
and we really saw it for the first time,

00:03:21.951 --> 00:03:23.995
actually, during our Dota 2 project.

00:03:23.995 --> 00:03:28.208
We started out with 16 cores
to train a little agent-

00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:31.961
on Jakub and Szymon who
were leading the project

00:03:31.961 --> 00:03:34.464
from an ML perspective, on their desktop.

00:03:34.464 --> 00:03:36.007
Then, they scaled to 32 cores.

00:03:36.007 --> 00:03:38.092
It felt like every week
I'd come back to the office

00:03:38.092 --> 00:03:40.178
and they'd scaled it by another 2x

00:03:40.178 --> 00:03:41.346
and we had 2x performance-

00:03:41.346 --> 00:03:43.389
just so clear, you just
need to keep going.

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:44.807
Where does this thing peter out?

00:03:44.807 --> 00:03:45.850
It just never did.

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:47.143
Founders get too much credit,

00:03:47.143 --> 00:03:49.395
because you have an initial product

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:50.730
that's a pretty reasonable idea

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:51.898
Then you listen to the customers,

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:52.815
and you follow what's working.

00:03:52.815 --> 00:03:55.276
So you were saying that was kind of OpenAI

00:03:55.276 --> 00:03:56.527
with the scaling hypothesis,

00:03:56.527 --> 00:04:00.406
where you started trying
to make Dota AIs work

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:03.743
and you noticed that adding
more compute worked really well,

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:05.036
and you said, "Where else

00:04:05.036 --> 00:04:07.413
will just throwing more
compute at it yield benefits.

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:10.166
I think that's to the first order correct.

00:04:10.166 --> 00:04:13.002
I think one thing that
distinguishes OpenAI

00:04:13.002 --> 00:04:15.630
from the typical startup,

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:18.383
is we did everything in reverse, right?

00:04:18.383 --> 00:04:21.344
It's like, you're supposed
to have a problem to solve.

00:04:21.344 --> 00:04:22.345
No one cares about the technology.

00:04:22.345 --> 00:04:23.471
Form the entity upfront.

00:04:23.471 --> 00:04:24.806
Exactly, yes.

00:04:24.806 --> 00:04:27.392
And for us, we really
chased the technology

00:04:27.392 --> 00:04:30.603
without any idea of how
it would be applied.

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:33.439
A lot of pursuing the technology

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:35.441
really is you have to let reality hit you

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:36.526
cold, hard in the face.

00:04:36.526 --> 00:04:39.696
There's just no other
way to achieve results.

00:04:39.696 --> 00:04:41.823
You can't will it into existence.

00:04:41.823 --> 00:04:43.241
You can't convince people

00:04:43.241 --> 00:04:44.075
that this is the thing.

00:04:44.075 --> 00:04:46.494
It's like, you have to
actually, make the system work.

00:04:46.494 --> 00:04:48.162
We have to just sort of figure out

00:04:48.162 --> 00:04:50.206
what is the right frontier,
what are the problems,

00:04:50.206 --> 00:04:52.333
what are the things that
are on the edge of working,

00:04:52.333 --> 00:04:54.043
and to really double down on those.

00:04:54.043 --> 00:04:55.420
What else do you take
away from the Dota work?

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:57.547
How else does it... Because you could

00:04:57.547 --> 00:04:58.965
have just started with LLMs

00:04:58.965 --> 00:04:59.924
and you know, we could have skipped

00:04:59.924 --> 00:05:01.426
that period in the wilderness,

00:05:01.426 --> 00:05:02.760
but it sounds like it
was somewhat formative

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.095
for the OpenAI organization.

00:05:04.095 --> 00:05:06.597
I think Dota had many lessons,

00:05:06.597 --> 00:05:10.351
one of which actually was
a management lesson for me.

00:05:10.351 --> 00:05:11.936
I remember when we
started out the project,

00:05:11.936 --> 00:05:14.772
I tried to set a list
of milestones, right?

00:05:14.772 --> 00:05:16.566
It's like, "Okay, this date
we're gonna beat this player.

00:05:16.566 --> 00:05:19.027
This date, we're gonna beat this player."

00:05:19.027 --> 00:05:21.279
That didn't work?
It did not work at all.

00:05:21.279 --> 00:05:23.573
I remember our first milestone came and

00:05:23.573 --> 00:05:25.533
Went?
Exactly.

00:05:25.533 --> 00:05:27.160
And so you realize that

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.078
you cannot control the outcome, right?

00:05:29.078 --> 00:05:31.164
You cannot set outcome-based milestones.

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:33.458
What you can do is you
can control the inputs

00:05:33.458 --> 00:05:35.585
of we're gonna try these
experiments by this date.

00:05:35.585 --> 00:05:37.837
We're going to implement
this feature by this date.

00:05:37.837 --> 00:05:39.505
And that is what actually worked.

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:41.799
And I remember it was one of those things

00:05:41.799 --> 00:05:45.345
that was just a story
that I could not have

00:05:45.345 --> 00:05:48.639
written in any better,
if we'd intended to.

00:05:48.639 --> 00:05:52.352
We beat our in-house best player

00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:54.854
and then we were playing as a semi-pro

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:56.939
and he was just trouncing
us, trouncing us.

00:05:56.939 --> 00:05:58.900
Then, suddenly we're
starting to get pretty good.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.110
So we showed up at the international,

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:02.653
this tournament blind.

00:06:02.653 --> 00:06:04.906
First day, we had three
players that we played against.

00:06:04.906 --> 00:06:08.368
We went 3-0, 3-0, and then 2-1.

00:06:08.368 --> 00:06:09.494
We're like, "Oh no. We lost.

00:06:09.494 --> 00:06:10.328
What happened?"

00:06:10.328 --> 00:06:12.413
It turned out that this pro
that we're playing against,

00:06:12.413 --> 00:06:14.665
he had used an item we'd
never trained against.

00:06:14.665 --> 00:06:15.583
And we were like, "Oh no,

00:06:15.583 --> 00:06:17.335
we're totally going to be hosed."

00:06:17.335 --> 00:06:18.378
So, what do we do?

00:06:18.378 --> 00:06:20.129
Well, we just need to change the training.

00:06:20.129 --> 00:06:22.965
And so people stayed up
all night to get this done.

00:06:22.965 --> 00:06:25.843
They added this extra item in there.

00:06:25.843 --> 00:06:28.137
4am, they finally get the job running.

00:06:28.137 --> 00:06:28.971
That Wednesday, we're supposed to play

00:06:28.971 --> 00:06:30.598
against the number two

00:06:30.598 --> 00:06:32.183
and the number one player in the world,

00:06:32.183 --> 00:06:34.227
and our semi-pro plays against it.

00:06:34.227 --> 00:06:35.895
He's like, "This bot is totally broken."

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:37.855
And we're like, "Oh no,
we like clearly had a bug.

00:06:37.855 --> 00:06:39.190
Something terrible has happened."

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:41.234
He was like, "Look, it's
taking all this damage

00:06:41.234 --> 00:06:42.068
it doesn't need to.

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:42.902
I'm gonna go kill it."

00:06:42.902 --> 00:06:44.320
He goes to kill it, he loses.

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:45.905
And he was like, "That was weird."

00:06:45.905 --> 00:06:47.782
He'd realized that what had happened was

00:06:47.782 --> 00:06:49.700
it had learned a baiting strategy.

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:51.327
And then we realized,
"Well, we have a super-bot,

00:06:51.327 --> 00:06:53.204
but it's so bad at the beginning,

00:06:53.204 --> 00:06:54.789
because it's trying to do the baiting.

00:06:54.789 --> 00:06:57.250
So, what if we just stitch the
two bots we have together?"

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:59.794
And then that bot was just undefeatable,

00:06:59.794 --> 00:07:02.088
and we played against this
number one player and won.

00:07:02.088 --> 00:07:03.881
To me this is like the story of

00:07:03.881 --> 00:07:05.091
how deep learning works, right?

00:07:05.091 --> 00:07:06.759
Is it's like you kind of can't control

00:07:06.759 --> 00:07:07.802
where you're gonna go.

00:07:07.802 --> 00:07:09.345
You can control everything that goes in.

00:07:09.345 --> 00:07:11.347
You can put these metrics
in these measurements

00:07:11.347 --> 00:07:13.808
and you can have sort of the evaluations.

00:07:13.808 --> 00:07:15.935
And being able to gauge where you're at

00:07:15.935 --> 00:07:17.270
is almost as important as being able

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:18.104
to make the forward progress.

00:07:18.104 --> 00:07:19.856
But if you get all those elements right,

00:07:19.856 --> 00:07:21.232
then you can do true magic.

00:07:21.232 --> 00:07:22.358
You're also describing something

00:07:22.358 --> 00:07:24.110
that worked really well
for an organization

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.320
where it was motivating
to stay up all night.

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:29.490
If the prize was impossibly far away,

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:31.617
it wouldn't have been as motivating.

00:07:31.617 --> 00:07:33.828
But the fact that there was
a near-term reward function

00:07:33.828 --> 00:07:35.538
and you were able to
show concrete progress...

00:07:35.538 --> 00:07:36.372
I think so.

00:07:36.372 --> 00:07:38.040
I think some of

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:40.585
my favorite engineering stories
have the same character.

00:07:40.585 --> 00:07:42.253
I remember you and I staying up all night

00:07:42.253 --> 00:07:44.589
to get our ISO 8583 integration.

00:07:44.589 --> 00:07:45.673
There's something about
staying up all night

00:07:45.673 --> 00:07:47.633
for like critical projects that

00:07:47.633 --> 00:07:48.468
actually have

00:07:48.468 --> 00:07:49.302
important history in

00:07:49.302 --> 00:07:50.136
all startups.

00:07:50.136 --> 00:07:52.597
I'm glad to hear that tradition
is alive and well at OpenAI.

00:07:52.597 --> 00:07:57.310
So, Dota has fallen, Chess
has fallen, Go has fallen.

00:07:58.186 --> 00:08:00.730
We've passed the Turing test,

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:05.443
I think by anyone's measure.

00:08:05.443 --> 00:08:06.277
People comment on

00:08:06.277 --> 00:08:07.987
how there was little fanfare when we did,

00:08:07.987 --> 00:08:09.572
but we seem to have
pretty clearly done so.

00:08:09.572 --> 00:08:11.616
What's a good new Turing test?

00:08:11.616 --> 00:08:13.034
Well, I'll tell you two things.

00:08:13.034 --> 00:08:14.285
One, is that, if you look at

00:08:14.285 --> 00:08:15.745
the strict version of the Turing test,

00:08:15.745 --> 00:08:17.455
I would actually claim
we haven't done it yet.

00:08:17.455 --> 00:08:19.332
No one's really gone that extra mile

00:08:19.332 --> 00:08:20.958
to say, "Can we actually have an AI

00:08:20.958 --> 00:08:23.294
that like is fully
indistinguishable from a human?"

00:08:23.294 --> 00:08:25.630
It's not clear if it's
even a good task, right?

00:08:25.630 --> 00:08:27.840
But I think that the right
question to your point is like,

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.426
well, what is the milestone

00:08:30.426 --> 00:08:32.637
that we should be chasing
in terms of capability?

00:08:32.637 --> 00:08:35.348
I remember talking to one of
our board members in 2018,

00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:39.644
and he said, you know,
"Look, I get that you

00:08:39.644 --> 00:08:41.354
were all excited about near-term AGI,

00:08:41.354 --> 00:08:44.023
but it just doesn't feel
like it's on track."

00:08:44.023 --> 00:08:45.191
I asked, "Well, what do you mean?"

00:08:45.191 --> 00:08:47.235
He said, "In a world with near-term AGI,

00:08:47.235 --> 00:08:49.237
you would expect massive economic value

00:08:49.237 --> 00:08:52.657
to be delivered by AI
already, and where is it?"

00:08:52.657 --> 00:08:55.117
In 2018, I think that was
a very fair criticism,

00:08:55.117 --> 00:08:57.537
and clearly, that's
starting to change now.

00:08:57.537 --> 00:08:59.038
It feels like one thing that

00:09:00.039 --> 00:09:03.251
may really change the AI
market is personalization.

00:09:03.251 --> 00:09:07.838
Up to quite recently, when
you asked ChatGPT a question,

00:09:07.838 --> 00:09:10.591
it was like walking into
a shop off the street.

00:09:10.591 --> 00:09:11.425
They've never met you before.

00:09:11.425 --> 00:09:12.635
They know nothing about you, whatever.

00:09:12.635 --> 00:09:15.096
That's obviously, not
ideal for this, you know,

00:09:15.096 --> 00:09:16.430
close part of your digital life.

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:18.099
I'm curious how you're
thinking about personalization

00:09:18.099 --> 00:09:19.225
from a product point of view,

00:09:19.225 --> 00:09:20.768
because it feels to me like

00:09:20.768 --> 00:09:24.647
the most meaningful change
since the chat interface.

00:09:25.898 --> 00:09:26.774
Two and a half years ago.

00:09:26.774 --> 00:09:28.442
Two and a half years ago.

00:09:28.442 --> 00:09:29.860
I mean, I think it's absolutely critical,

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:32.446
and I think it is very rightly considered

00:09:32.446 --> 00:09:33.906
to be kind of a next frontier.

00:09:33.906 --> 00:09:38.411
I'm someone who always, when
I just Google something,

00:09:38.411 --> 00:09:40.454
I go into Incognito Mode,

00:09:40.454 --> 00:09:41.539
because I don't even want my computer

00:09:41.539 --> 00:09:42.915
to remember that history.

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:45.668
And I always used to go for
temporary chats on ChatGPT.

00:09:45.668 --> 00:09:47.378
But now my usage has totally reversed.

00:09:47.378 --> 00:09:49.505
I want ChatGPT to remember everything.

00:09:49.505 --> 00:09:51.215
I want it to remember my interactions,

00:09:51.215 --> 00:09:52.425
because it's useful.

00:09:52.425 --> 00:09:53.342
Okay. So you guys figured out,

00:09:53.342 --> 00:09:54.427
from a product point of view

00:09:54.427 --> 00:09:56.137
how to make the memory
actually work better.

00:09:56.137 --> 00:09:58.306
It's the product point of view,

00:09:58.306 --> 00:10:00.099
but also, really the
research point of view.

00:10:00.099 --> 00:10:01.767
And I presume there's a flip
flop between the product

00:10:01.767 --> 00:10:03.519
and research where,
when you find something

00:10:03.519 --> 00:10:05.396
that's useful from a
product point of view,

00:10:05.396 --> 00:10:06.230
then the product people say,

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:07.356
"I'm just a product person,

00:10:07.356 --> 00:10:09.275
you researchers go
actually make this good."

00:10:09.275 --> 00:10:10.860
Then that kind of kicks off more research.

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:11.694
Is that how it works?

00:10:11.694 --> 00:10:13.487
To some extent

00:10:13.487 --> 00:10:15.323
that's a failure mode in our mind.

00:10:15.323 --> 00:10:16.782
I think that we really

00:10:16.782 --> 00:10:18.284
don't want to have that kind of silo.

00:10:18.284 --> 00:10:19.869
We really want to blur the lines

00:10:19.869 --> 00:10:21.245
and have people cross-collaborate.

00:10:21.245 --> 00:10:22.747
And so, it's avery different mindset from

00:10:22.747 --> 00:10:24.206
how you would traditionally
build a product

00:10:24.206 --> 00:10:26.083
versus how you do research.

00:10:26.083 --> 00:10:27.460
Part of what had happened, actually,

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:29.086
was that we had GPT-3,

00:10:29.086 --> 00:10:31.297
we knew we needed to build a product

00:10:31.297 --> 00:10:33.215
in order to be able to
continue to raise funding,

00:10:33.215 --> 00:10:35.301
and we were like,

00:10:35.301 --> 00:10:36.510
"Well, what product do we build?

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:37.553
And we wrote down a list of,

00:10:37.553 --> 00:10:38.888
like, 100 different products.

00:10:38.888 --> 00:10:40.806
We could do a medical
thing. Then you're like,

00:10:40.806 --> 00:10:43.601
"Okay. Well, now we have
to sell to hospitals.

00:10:43.601 --> 00:10:44.769
We're gonna have to hire doctors."

00:10:44.769 --> 00:10:47.772
You realize you give up
on the G in AGI, right?

00:10:47.772 --> 00:10:49.774
You're gonna like go for a specific thing.

00:10:49.774 --> 00:10:53.527
Someone had the idea of saying,

00:10:53.527 --> 00:10:56.405
"Well, why don't we just make a API

00:10:56.405 --> 00:10:58.074
and let people figure it out?

00:10:58.074 --> 00:10:58.908
And, again,

00:10:58.908 --> 00:10:59.825
this is totally backwards

00:10:59.825 --> 00:11:01.452
from how you're supposed
to do a startup, right?

00:11:01.452 --> 00:11:03.204
You're supposed to have a problem,

00:11:03.204 --> 00:11:04.246
and we had no idea what the problem was.

00:11:04.246 --> 00:11:06.290
Yeah, yeah, yeah, we're
gonna back into the problem.

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:07.416
And so this actually felt like this was

00:11:07.416 --> 00:11:09.251
probably the hardest
project that I've ever done,

00:11:09.251 --> 00:11:10.961
because it felt totally doomed, right?

00:11:10.961 --> 00:11:13.214
It's like, I know like every instinct,

00:11:13.214 --> 00:11:14.340
every builder instinct of mine says-

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:15.299
Did it actually feel doomed?

00:11:15.299 --> 00:11:16.300
Oh, it felt totally doomed.

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:19.095
It wasn't just like
open-ended or something?

00:11:19.095 --> 00:11:19.929
No, it felt doomed.

00:11:19.929 --> 00:11:21.222
But you were still doing it.

00:11:21.222 --> 00:11:22.807
Yeah. I mean, it's like, at some point,

00:11:22.807 --> 00:11:25.810
if you have... There was
definitely no other path.

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:26.644
There was no other path.

00:11:26.644 --> 00:11:28.646
It was the only shot we had.

00:11:28.646 --> 00:11:30.690
I remember someone also saying like,

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:33.192
"I can't imagine anyone paying
for samples from this model."

00:11:33.192 --> 00:11:35.069
And I was like, "You might be right."

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:37.446
I'm still trying to imagine it myself.

00:11:37.446 --> 00:11:39.657
Yes. It was just not clear,

00:11:39.657 --> 00:11:41.742
were we above threshold
or below threshold?

00:11:41.742 --> 00:11:43.661
We showed it to people,

00:11:43.661 --> 00:11:44.829
and people were interested.

00:11:44.829 --> 00:11:46.539
But that's very different
from people being like,

00:11:46.539 --> 00:11:48.416
"I will build my company on top of this,"

00:11:48.416 --> 00:11:51.585
Yes. So, what was the first
use case to get any traction?

00:11:51.585 --> 00:11:52.420
AI Dungeon.

00:11:52.420 --> 00:11:54.255
What was that again?

00:11:54.255 --> 00:11:55.131
There you go.

00:11:56.132 --> 00:12:00.469
AI Dungeon was a
text-based adventure game.

00:12:00.469 --> 00:12:01.887
Oh sure, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:12:01.887 --> 00:12:04.140
Okay. Bbut that was real revenue

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:05.558
or that was non-zero revenue?

00:12:05.558 --> 00:12:06.392
It was enough.

00:12:06.392 --> 00:12:07.935
And, in fact, I believe

00:12:07.935 --> 00:12:09.603
they were our first paying user.

00:12:09.603 --> 00:12:10.896
And that gets you
confused for, you're like,

00:12:10.896 --> 00:12:13.607
"Ah, clearly the future of
OpenAI is gaming" you know?

00:12:13.607 --> 00:12:14.984
I know, back to our roots.

00:12:14.984 --> 00:12:15.943
Exactly.

00:12:15.943 --> 00:12:18.487
It's interesting too,

00:12:18.487 --> 00:12:21.240
because we had dreamed of
all of these applications,

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:23.701
like medicine and all these things,

00:12:23.701 --> 00:12:25.536
and you start with the gaming application.

00:12:25.536 --> 00:12:28.205
But we could see signs of
life on so many other things.

00:12:28.205 --> 00:12:30.249
I think in many ways GPT-3 was like

00:12:30.249 --> 00:12:32.209
the world's best demo machine, right?

00:12:32.209 --> 00:12:33.669
When we released the API,

00:12:33.669 --> 00:12:35.629
people were coming with all
these cool things you could do,

00:12:35.629 --> 00:12:38.591
but making them reliable was so hard.

00:12:38.591 --> 00:12:41.427
It really wasn't until the
next generation of GPT-4,

00:12:41.427 --> 00:12:43.512
until we started to figure out
how to do post training well

00:12:43.512 --> 00:12:45.097
that then you were actually able to build

00:12:45.097 --> 00:12:47.016
real businesses on top of these things.

00:12:47.016 --> 00:12:48.768
Bill Gates was saying recently that GPT-4

00:12:48.768 --> 00:12:52.104
was the best demo he'd
ever seen since Xerox Park.

00:12:52.104 --> 00:12:53.522
You know this quote?
Yes.

00:12:53.522 --> 00:12:55.149
He said it to me the night that he saw it.

00:12:56.859 --> 00:13:00.029
Yeah. That's high praise.

00:13:00.988 --> 00:13:02.156
I wanna touch the medicine thing,

00:13:02.156 --> 00:13:02.990
because you've mentioned it.

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:04.742
Like you said

00:13:04.742 --> 00:13:07.495
your family has personal stories.

00:13:07.495 --> 00:13:11.874
You've talked about getting
very valuable diagnostic help.

00:13:11.874 --> 00:13:15.127
We, ourselves, actually, it's
much more minor in our family,

00:13:15.127 --> 00:13:16.754
but we managed to fix a cat

00:13:16.754 --> 00:13:19.298
thanks to debugging it with an LLM.

00:13:19.298 --> 00:13:21.967
I think that's an interesting example,

00:13:21.967 --> 00:13:24.386
because so many people that I know

00:13:24.386 --> 00:13:26.889
have had some kind of
experience like this.

00:13:26.889 --> 00:13:28.808
Maybe it's because you actually

00:13:28.808 --> 00:13:32.478
don't get that much time from a doctor.

00:13:32.478 --> 00:13:36.315
Are there other examples like
this medicine application

00:13:36.315 --> 00:13:38.192
where you're seeing a lot of success,

00:13:39.151 --> 00:13:40.986
that many people have similar stories,

00:13:40.986 --> 00:13:42.822
but we just hear less about?

00:13:42.822 --> 00:13:45.199
Yeah, I think it's a great question.

00:13:45.199 --> 00:13:47.827
And by the way, I think
like medicine is an example

00:13:47.827 --> 00:13:50.746
of one where I kind of
thought it was gonna be

00:13:50.746 --> 00:13:51.872
one of the last domains that

00:13:51.872 --> 00:13:53.457
we would successfully
be able to add value in,

00:13:53.457 --> 00:13:55.000
but it turns out that the bar is so low,

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:56.210
you just need to exceed WebMD.

00:13:56.210 --> 00:13:59.338
I think that we have seen

00:13:59.338 --> 00:14:01.215
other areas that are
like a real common theme.

00:14:01.215 --> 00:14:02.550
One that's very interesting right now

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:03.509
is the life coach,

00:14:03.509 --> 00:14:05.594
life advice kind of application,

00:14:05.594 --> 00:14:06.887
where you just talk your AI.

00:14:06.887 --> 00:14:07.721
That's actually really taking off.

00:14:07.721 --> 00:14:08.305
Yeah. It really is.

00:14:08.305 --> 00:14:12.935
Education is another area

00:14:12.935 --> 00:14:15.855
that just like clearly is
really having an impact.

00:14:15.855 --> 00:14:17.356
There are studies coming out now

00:14:17.356 --> 00:14:20.651
that actually show that people
are able to learn better

00:14:20.651 --> 00:14:22.236
through the use of these tools.

00:14:22.236 --> 00:14:23.112
That's to be expected, right?

00:14:23.112 --> 00:14:25.906
It is the Bloom 2 sigma
effect in a product.

00:14:25.906 --> 00:14:28.659
Yes. And that, for example is like why,

00:14:28.659 --> 00:14:31.537
Sal Khan, started Khan Academy,

00:14:31.537 --> 00:14:32.371
to think about

00:14:32.371 --> 00:14:34.206
if you can give personalized
tutoring to everyone.

00:14:34.206 --> 00:14:35.249
We showed him GPT-4. He's like,

00:14:35.249 --> 00:14:36.208
"This is the thing.

00:14:36.208 --> 00:14:38.419
We need to become a GPT-4 app."

00:14:38.419 --> 00:14:39.879
I think that there are

00:14:39.879 --> 00:14:42.298
these really amazing applications

00:14:42.298 --> 00:14:45.259
that are affecting everyone's daily lives.

00:14:45.259 --> 00:14:46.844
Obviously, programming is another one,

00:14:46.844 --> 00:14:48.012
that people are seeing

00:14:48.012 --> 00:14:50.514
all across the board in
a professional context.

00:14:50.514 --> 00:14:51.515
We're heading to a world

00:14:51.515 --> 00:14:53.309
where, like,

00:14:53.309 --> 00:14:56.228
if you want to do productive work,

00:14:56.228 --> 00:14:57.605
and you don't have access to computer,

00:14:57.605 --> 00:14:58.981
you're going to be hampered.

00:14:58.981 --> 00:15:01.025
Similarly, not having access to AI,

00:15:01.025 --> 00:15:02.484
it's heading in the same direction.

00:15:02.484 --> 00:15:04.862
Speaking of not having access to AI,

00:15:05.779 --> 00:15:10.075
I will posit that these
days, it feels like

00:15:10.075 --> 00:15:14.997
AI product development
is mostly OS limited.

00:15:14.997 --> 00:15:16.540
Is that how you feel?

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:18.125
Are we stuck at the moment?

00:15:18.125 --> 00:15:20.336
I do feel a little of stuckage,

00:15:20.336 --> 00:15:23.964
but not to worry, it was overcomeable.

00:15:23.964 --> 00:15:25.674
But yeah, I think it is true.

00:15:25.674 --> 00:15:29.178
Two years ago we released
plug-ins in ChatGPT.

00:15:29.178 --> 00:15:30.346
Do you remember those?
Yeah.

00:15:30.346 --> 00:15:33.599
That was trying to make it

00:15:33.599 --> 00:15:36.936
so anyone could write apps
that then ChatGPT could access.

00:15:36.936 --> 00:15:38.854
The models were just not that good, right?

00:15:38.854 --> 00:15:41.190
That we limited to like
three plug-ins at a time.

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:43.233
You could have only so
many functions and stuff.

00:15:43.233 --> 00:15:45.152
It just wasn't that reliable.

00:15:45.152 --> 00:15:48.322
Now we're in a world where MCP basically

00:15:48.322 --> 00:15:49.323
is really taking off

00:15:49.323 --> 00:15:54.328
and is a way to hook up
your AI to different tools,

00:15:54.954 --> 00:15:56.538
and very much like kind of trying to take

00:15:56.538 --> 00:15:59.416
that same type of idea
and really make it work.

00:15:59.416 --> 00:16:01.543
Now, the world that
we're in is very similar,

00:16:01.543 --> 00:16:03.462
where there's certain
interfaces we don't have.

00:16:03.462 --> 00:16:07.549
Being able to access your
phone and all those APIs.

00:16:07.549 --> 00:16:08.801
There's a question of,

00:16:08.801 --> 00:16:11.428
is the model above threshold
to actually use them or not?

00:16:11.428 --> 00:16:13.764
My observation has been that basically,

00:16:13.764 --> 00:16:15.808
I think that there is maybe
a lag of, like, six months

00:16:15.808 --> 00:16:18.602
of different interfaces
that are hard to access.

00:16:18.602 --> 00:16:20.980
But once we have a model
that's good enough,

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:21.939
we will find a way.

00:16:21.939 --> 00:16:23.315
People will find a way.

00:16:23.315 --> 00:16:24.775
I think that we're in a world

00:16:24.775 --> 00:16:27.528
where I have every
expectation that we will get

00:16:27.528 --> 00:16:28.946
the future that has been promised.

00:16:28.946 --> 00:16:30.489
It's just gonna take some work.

00:16:30.489 --> 00:16:31.699
I feel like there are many moments

00:16:31.699 --> 00:16:35.369
where I'm using my phone,
and I want a single button

00:16:35.369 --> 00:16:37.705
where it's just, like

00:16:37.705 --> 00:16:39.832
"ChatGPT what do you think of this?

00:16:39.832 --> 00:16:41.500
I need your comment. I
need your fact check.

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:43.335
I need your explanation."
Something like that.

00:16:43.335 --> 00:16:46.130
You take a screenshot, and
you like go into ChatGPT,

00:16:46.130 --> 00:16:48.090
you click "upload photo."

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:50.884
It feels very 1993

00:16:50.884 --> 00:16:53.012
versus the button on my
phone that just says,

00:16:53.012 --> 00:16:54.805
"Hey, ChatGPT, what do
you think about this?"

00:16:54.805 --> 00:16:57.641
Obviously, you guys are not
empowered to go build that.

00:16:57.641 --> 00:16:58.934
That's what I mean by it feels somehow

00:16:58.934 --> 00:17:00.561
like we're a little
operating system limited.

00:17:00.561 --> 00:17:01.895
I definitely get it,

00:17:01.895 --> 00:17:04.606
but I'll say, I think that
there are two dimensions.

00:17:04.606 --> 00:17:06.025
This is how I've been
thinking about things

00:17:06.025 --> 00:17:08.485
since we released the API back in 2020.

00:17:08.485 --> 00:17:10.779
There's capability and convenience.

00:17:11.989 --> 00:17:14.158
What you're referring to
is the convenience, right?

00:17:14.158 --> 00:17:15.492
It's like pretty inconvenient

00:17:15.492 --> 00:17:17.369
to do the screenshot and paste it.

00:17:17.369 --> 00:17:20.664
But the thing is, if the
capability is good enough,

00:17:20.664 --> 00:17:23.459
you are willing to accept any
sort of inconvenience, right?

00:17:23.459 --> 00:17:26.545
It's like if this, by
taking the screenshot

00:17:26.545 --> 00:17:27.588
and showing it to ChatGPT,

00:17:27.588 --> 00:17:29.298
it could give you amazing insight,

00:17:29.298 --> 00:17:30.716
it could tell you like how to, you know,

00:17:30.716 --> 00:17:32.176
build Stripe in some way,

00:17:32.176 --> 00:17:34.011
and it takes you like a month to do it,

00:17:34.011 --> 00:17:35.345
you have to crawl the top of the mountain,

00:17:35.345 --> 00:17:36.513
you'll do it, right?

00:17:36.513 --> 00:17:38.849
The convenience will not stop you.

00:17:38.849 --> 00:17:41.185
And so the point that I'm
trying to make is that

00:17:41.185 --> 00:17:43.228
if the capability is high enough,

00:17:43.228 --> 00:17:45.355
people will start doing a specific flow.

00:17:45.355 --> 00:17:46.523
They'll discover the use cases

00:17:46.523 --> 00:17:47.816
and the convenience will just catch up.

00:17:47.816 --> 00:17:48.650
In the convenience,

00:17:48.650 --> 00:17:49.693
there's so much pressure.

00:17:49.693 --> 00:17:50.986
There's pressure on
the phone manufacturer.

00:17:50.986 --> 00:17:52.905
There's pressure on us.
There's pressure on everyone

00:17:52.905 --> 00:17:54.239
in order to bring down the convenience.

00:17:54.239 --> 00:17:55.699
o, I just need to be patient,

00:17:55.699 --> 00:17:56.742
and it'll be great in three years time.

00:17:56.742 --> 00:17:59.870
Yes. And really lean in and use the AI.

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:02.706
A criticism people like to levy of AI is,

00:18:02.706 --> 00:18:04.500
"Yeah, it's great and handy and all,

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:09.505
but it hasn't come up with
a single novel advance

00:18:09.713 --> 00:18:12.299
in mathematics or science."
Have you?

00:18:12.299 --> 00:18:13.258
Well, you could have

00:18:13.258 --> 00:18:15.219
if you'd become a mathematician.

00:18:15.219 --> 00:18:18.180
But you know, but humanity has, you know,

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:19.556
for keeping the scoreboard.

00:18:20.557 --> 00:18:22.142
What do you make of that criticism?

00:18:22.142 --> 00:18:23.268
Just wait.

00:18:23.268 --> 00:18:27.439
Okay, so you think like take

00:18:27.439 --> 00:18:28.440
one of the Millennium Prizes

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:29.691
Do you think we plausibly will see that?

00:18:29.691 --> 00:18:30.776
I think for sure.

00:18:30.776 --> 00:18:31.944
I mean, there's no question.

00:18:31.944 --> 00:18:33.737
Two years, five years, 10 years?

00:18:33.737 --> 00:18:34.655
I think that is the question.

00:18:34.655 --> 00:18:35.572
It's just timing.

00:18:35.572 --> 00:18:36.782
That is my question.

00:18:37.866 --> 00:18:39.451
I mean, I would put two to five years

00:18:39.451 --> 00:18:41.745
as the right number.

00:18:41.745 --> 00:18:44.748
I think ultimately this comes back

00:18:44.748 --> 00:18:45.916
to the question of benchmarks, right?

00:18:45.916 --> 00:18:48.836
Actually being able to
solve a millennium problem

00:18:48.836 --> 00:18:50.212
is pretty high bar.

00:18:51.088 --> 00:18:52.381
Yeah.

00:18:52.381 --> 00:18:54.800
And once you can do that,
there's so many other things

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:56.510
that will definitely be possible.

00:18:56.510 --> 00:18:57.386
And I think that we're starting

00:18:57.386 --> 00:18:58.470
to see the leading edges of this.

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:02.432
And to me, if we look at
our definition of AGI...

00:19:02.432 --> 00:19:03.684
We recently started talking

00:19:03.684 --> 00:19:06.061
about this framework of
thinking about levels of AGI,

00:19:06.061 --> 00:19:09.982
starting from chatbots,
to reasoners, to agents,

00:19:09.982 --> 00:19:13.735
to innovators, to
organizations, Five levels.

00:19:13.735 --> 00:19:16.405
We're basically, somewhere
in level three right now.

00:19:16.405 --> 00:19:17.573
Level four,

00:19:17.573 --> 00:19:19.449
this innovator, like,
that's gonna be different.

00:19:19.449 --> 00:19:20.951
I recently posted some pictures

00:19:20.951 --> 00:19:22.744
of our visit to Abilene, Texas,

00:19:22.744 --> 00:19:24.913
where we're building these
big data centers together

00:19:24.913 --> 00:19:26.874
with our partner, Oracle.

00:19:26.874 --> 00:19:31.879
Imagine taking that whole data center

00:19:32.171 --> 00:19:34.590
and just thinking hard
about one problem, right?

00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:35.883
Imagine it just thinking about

00:19:35.883 --> 00:19:37.593
how to solve a Millennium Problem

00:19:37.593 --> 00:19:39.386
or how to cure a specific kind of cancer.

00:19:39.386 --> 00:19:41.096
Maybe it needs access to some apparatus.

00:19:41.096 --> 00:19:42.806
Maybe it needs access to robotic wet labs.

00:19:42.806 --> 00:19:43.849
Maybe it needs access

00:19:43.849 --> 00:19:45.267
to different tools in the world.

00:19:45.267 --> 00:19:48.645
But that level of computational power

00:19:48.645 --> 00:19:50.856
coupled with the ability to experiment

00:19:50.856 --> 00:19:52.691
and learn from your ideas,

00:19:52.691 --> 00:19:55.569
that is going to be something
the world has never seen.

00:19:55.569 --> 00:19:59.489
So yet again, we just haven't
put a respectable amount

00:19:59.489 --> 00:20:01.200
of compute on these problems

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:02.451
compared to what we will be doing.

00:20:02.451 --> 00:20:04.119
Yeah, we're still on these
tiny little computers.

00:20:04.119 --> 00:20:05.579
So, that actually gets to,

00:20:06.872 --> 00:20:09.875
in terms of these scaling laws,
do they eventually run out

00:20:09.875 --> 00:20:11.585
because we just run out of compute?

00:20:11.585 --> 00:20:13.712
Or do we eventually get to the point

00:20:13.712 --> 00:20:15.589
where we're inventing new
kinds of nuclear energy,

00:20:15.589 --> 00:20:18.050
and that is what unlocks the next level?

00:20:18.050 --> 00:20:21.553
A lot of energy that comes
online now is for data centers,

00:20:21.553 --> 00:20:25.140
which was not true when you
guys started training GPT-2.

00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:28.936
Isn't that the upcoming bottleneck?

00:20:28.936 --> 00:20:30.687
I mean it's as it should be, right?

00:20:30.687 --> 00:20:33.649
It really should be that it's energy

00:20:33.649 --> 00:20:35.359
manufactured into intelligence

00:20:35.359 --> 00:20:36.485
and that's your only bottleneck.

00:20:36.485 --> 00:20:39.154
But I'm saying that'll
be like quite a plateau

00:20:39.154 --> 00:20:41.281
compared to the exponential growth

00:20:41.281 --> 00:20:42.950
we've seen over the past few years.

00:20:44.284 --> 00:20:46.036
Unless things really change in terms of

00:20:46.036 --> 00:20:48.413
permitting and plans for
building everything like that.

00:20:48.413 --> 00:20:50.249
This is I think the core, right?

00:20:50.249 --> 00:20:54.294
If you look at every trend in this field,

00:20:54.294 --> 00:20:55.379
there are these exponentials,

00:20:55.379 --> 00:20:57.881
these S-curves that
sum up to exponentials.

00:20:57.881 --> 00:20:59.800
Sure. But these exponentials
were mostly existing

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:03.178
in like tech, Silicon Valley space

00:21:03.178 --> 00:21:05.430
where it was pretty easy
to have exponential growth.

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:08.475
It's pretty hard in
permitting and real estate

00:21:08.475 --> 00:21:11.395
and damming rivers and
building nuclear power plants.

00:21:11.395 --> 00:21:13.313
It's harder to have
exponential growth there.

00:21:13.313 --> 00:21:15.065
Well, let's see how fusion pans out.

00:21:15.065 --> 00:21:16.984
Yeah. Okay, but even fusion,

00:21:16.984 --> 00:21:18.902
most industry observers would say

00:21:18.902 --> 00:21:20.070
is still five years away.

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:20.904
And so, where does the next five years

00:21:20.904 --> 00:21:22.447
of power growth come from?

00:21:22.447 --> 00:21:24.992
I think that it is very possible

00:21:24.992 --> 00:21:27.119
that we end up bottlenecked on energy,

00:21:27.119 --> 00:21:28.745
and that's actually, one
reason that we've been spending

00:21:28.745 --> 00:21:31.290
a lot of time really trying
to advocate for the fact

00:21:31.290 --> 00:21:34.459
that we just need far more power.

00:21:34.459 --> 00:21:37.629
My observation of the
market is that ultimately,

00:21:37.629 --> 00:21:39.715
the capitalist markets do provide.

00:21:39.715 --> 00:21:42.301
I think there's this like
absolute tsunami of demand

00:21:42.301 --> 00:21:44.636
that is coming our way,

00:21:44.636 --> 00:21:48.223
but I feel some confidence
that, again, there's like,

00:21:48.223 --> 00:21:49.474
when there's enough pressure,

00:21:49.474 --> 00:21:51.935
when there's enough clarity
of, this is the bottleneck...

00:21:51.935 --> 00:21:54.062
And it's not just for any company, right?

00:21:54.062 --> 00:21:56.606
It's really for national competitiveness.

00:21:56.606 --> 00:21:57.649
You look at other countries

00:21:57.649 --> 00:22:00.152
that are just building
huge amounts of power,

00:22:00.152 --> 00:22:01.987
far more than we are.

00:22:01.987 --> 00:22:04.531
I think that actually for
America to remain competitive,

00:22:04.531 --> 00:22:06.074
there's just no choice but to build it.

00:22:06.074 --> 00:22:06.908
We've got to figure out power.

00:22:06.908 --> 00:22:08.785
We do.
Speaking of bottlenecks,

00:22:08.785 --> 00:22:12.205
everyone was talking about
the data wall in 2023.

00:22:12.205 --> 00:22:13.290
I think this is an interesting thing,

00:22:13.290 --> 00:22:15.792
where no one is talking
about the data wall anymore,

00:22:15.792 --> 00:22:20.088
and yet, it doesn't feel like
AI progress has slowed down.

00:22:20.088 --> 00:22:22.049
Is it just test-time compute?

00:22:22.049 --> 00:22:24.760
Is it, like, people were
wrong about the data wall

00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:26.178
and where it presented a bottleneck?

00:22:26.178 --> 00:22:28.263
Is there actually still a data wall,

00:22:28.263 --> 00:22:29.514
but it's two years away?

00:22:29.514 --> 00:22:30.974
It's basically, all of
these things, right?

00:22:30.974 --> 00:22:32.142
It truly is.

00:22:32.142 --> 00:22:34.269
It's like, you keep changing the paradigm.

00:22:34.269 --> 00:22:36.104
That is the real core of

00:22:36.104 --> 00:22:37.522
the Kurzweil view of the world,

00:22:37.522 --> 00:22:41.485
is that, fine, this one way
of doing things taps out.

00:22:41.485 --> 00:22:43.445
If you just look at that
one way of doing things,

00:22:43.445 --> 00:22:44.821
you feel hopeless.

00:22:44.821 --> 00:22:45.864
You feel like this is it,

00:22:45.864 --> 00:22:48.241
but somehow you will find a new S-curve.

00:22:48.241 --> 00:22:49.117
And I think that's what's happened.

00:22:49.117 --> 00:22:50.243
For example, synthetic data,

00:22:50.243 --> 00:22:51.620
for example, reinforcement
learning, right?

00:22:51.620 --> 00:22:53.455
If you think about the RL paradigm,

00:22:53.455 --> 00:22:56.041
fundamentally that's a data
production mechanism, right?

00:22:56.041 --> 00:22:57.584
And it just that the AI

00:22:57.584 --> 00:22:59.336
happens to be training on its own data

00:22:59.336 --> 00:23:00.754
and then you learn it very rapidly,

00:23:00.754 --> 00:23:02.464
and then you learn on that.

00:23:02.464 --> 00:23:05.217
Each of these has taken us much further.

00:23:05.217 --> 00:23:07.135
I think there are lots
of algorithmic ideas,

00:23:07.135 --> 00:23:08.470
lots of techniques, lots of ways

00:23:08.470 --> 00:23:10.680
of even using the existing data better.

00:23:11.640 --> 00:23:15.435
I think that fundamentally
the S-curves continue,

00:23:15.435 --> 00:23:18.397
and if you zoom out, it all
looks smooth and uninterrupted.

00:23:19.356 --> 00:23:20.816
So, it's kinda like chip miniaturization

00:23:20.816 --> 00:23:22.859
where each generation people are like,

00:23:22.859 --> 00:23:23.902
"Okay, well, that's the smallest

00:23:23.902 --> 00:23:25.070
you can possibly make a chip."

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:25.904
Do you know what I mean?

00:23:25.904 --> 00:23:27.531
"That's it we're done
with miniaturization,"

00:23:27.531 --> 00:23:28.365
and somehow we figure out a way.

00:23:28.365 --> 00:23:30.117
Yes. Now one difference with chips

00:23:30.117 --> 00:23:30.992
is at the end of the day,

00:23:30.992 --> 00:23:33.120
there is some limit.

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:35.163
Right. But we've never
been that close to that.

00:23:35.163 --> 00:23:35.997
Yes.

00:23:37.124 --> 00:23:39.167
Where does AI coding go?

00:23:39.167 --> 00:23:44.172
In particular, vibe coding
is all the rage right now.

00:23:44.631 --> 00:23:48.218
It's kind of the term of 2025.

00:23:48.218 --> 00:23:51.263
It's sort of working.
It's very impressive.

00:23:51.263 --> 00:23:56.268
No one is really fully
letting AI software engineers

00:23:56.518 --> 00:23:58.061
run end-to-end in production.

00:23:58.061 --> 00:23:58.895
I'm just curious,

00:23:58.895 --> 00:24:00.647
what are your one to two-year predictions

00:24:00.647 --> 00:24:02.315
on what happens with AI coding?

00:24:02.315 --> 00:24:04.109
Well, my general observation is that

00:24:04.109 --> 00:24:06.361
once something kind of
works in this field,

00:24:06.361 --> 00:24:07.904
the next gen is gonna be great.

00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:10.782
I think that's where we are
right now for AI coding.

00:24:10.782 --> 00:24:12.993
I think what we're going to see

00:24:12.993 --> 00:24:16.288
is AIs taking more and
more of the drudgery,

00:24:16.288 --> 00:24:18.957
more of this like pain, more of the parts

00:24:18.957 --> 00:24:20.750
that are not very fun for humans.

00:24:20.750 --> 00:24:22.294
Now, one thing that's
very interesting is that

00:24:22.294 --> 00:24:24.796
I think that so far, the vibe coding

00:24:24.796 --> 00:24:26.214
has actually taken a lot of code

00:24:26.214 --> 00:24:27.424
that is actually quite fun

00:24:27.424 --> 00:24:29.342
and left behind the
review and the deployment,

00:24:29.342 --> 00:24:31.136
these things that are not fun at all.

00:24:31.136 --> 00:24:32.471
I'm hopeful that we're actually

00:24:32.471 --> 00:24:33.930
gonna be able to make a lot of progress

00:24:33.930 --> 00:24:35.849
on these other areas as well,

00:24:35.849 --> 00:24:37.809
but fundamentally, we should really end up

00:24:37.809 --> 00:24:39.478
with a full AI coworker.

00:24:39.478 --> 00:24:41.813
And I think it really will be
anything you want to create,

00:24:41.813 --> 00:24:43.315
you can be the manager,

00:24:43.315 --> 00:24:44.149
and you can have

00:24:44.149 --> 00:24:45.984
this team of software engineering agents.

00:24:45.984 --> 00:24:47.736
Now, the thing that I think will be

00:24:47.736 --> 00:24:48.904
very interesting to see is,

00:24:48.904 --> 00:24:51.448
is there a world where
the AI becomes the manager

00:24:51.448 --> 00:24:52.741
and it gives you ideas,

00:24:52.741 --> 00:24:54.159
gives you some tasks to do?

00:24:54.159 --> 00:24:55.327
That's something that, again,

00:24:55.327 --> 00:24:56.912
it's just totally backwards

00:24:56.912 --> 00:24:58.538
in terms of how we think about it.

00:24:58.538 --> 00:25:00.957
But are there ways in
which you can actually

00:25:00.957 --> 00:25:04.127
have outcomes for companies

00:25:04.127 --> 00:25:05.295
and actually have people whose jobs

00:25:05.295 --> 00:25:06.463
become much more meaningful

00:25:06.463 --> 00:25:09.090
because they have an AI who
really deeply understands them

00:25:09.090 --> 00:25:10.509
in the same way that your

00:25:10.509 --> 00:25:14.804
AI doctor really deeply
understands all of your needs.

00:25:14.804 --> 00:25:16.139
But isn't part of the common thread

00:25:16.139 --> 00:25:17.265
that we're talking about here,

00:25:17.265 --> 00:25:20.852
often places where AI tools underperform,

00:25:20.852 --> 00:25:23.522
it's because they're trying
to do something generally,

00:25:23.522 --> 00:25:25.941
Like, voice recognition is not that good

00:25:25.941 --> 00:25:27.651
because it's trying to
recognize all voices

00:25:27.651 --> 00:25:28.985
as opposed to trying to recognize

00:25:28.985 --> 00:25:30.070
my voice in particular.

00:25:30.070 --> 00:25:33.031
Similarly, with AI coding,
they work well in places

00:25:33.031 --> 00:25:36.451
where you need no context at all

00:25:36.451 --> 00:25:39.037
and we're single-shotting an app based on

00:25:39.037 --> 00:25:40.830
publicly available libraries.

00:25:40.830 --> 00:25:43.917
In places where you have to understand

00:25:43.917 --> 00:25:45.752
a million-line code base,

00:25:45.752 --> 00:25:50.173
they haven't fully figured out
how to do a good job of that.

00:25:50.173 --> 00:25:51.633
Is that a fair parallel

00:25:51.633 --> 00:25:52.717
to draw between all these challenges?

00:25:52.717 --> 00:25:54.678
Well, I think there are
two things in there.

00:25:54.678 --> 00:25:57.556
One is that I think this
is already changing, right?

00:25:57.556 --> 00:25:59.015
So if you look at something like Codex,

00:25:59.015 --> 00:26:02.227
it's actually great at
operating in a big codebase.

00:26:02.227 --> 00:26:04.813
Like I ask it for where
functionality is implemented

00:26:04.813 --> 00:26:07.440
and it's better than I am at finding it.

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:09.150
Which is kind of a wild fact.

00:26:09.150 --> 00:26:11.236
It's super cool to see
it like grepping around

00:26:11.236 --> 00:26:14.155
and just going and exploring.

00:26:14.155 --> 00:26:15.240
Actually, this is one thing

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:16.575
that we really shot for with Codex,

00:26:16.575 --> 00:26:19.286
was to build a tool for software engineers

00:26:19.286 --> 00:26:21.830
who are not necessarily vibe coding.

00:26:21.830 --> 00:26:23.498
It's not about building
a new app from scratch,

00:26:23.498 --> 00:26:24.624
which is a cool demo,

00:26:24.624 --> 00:26:25.709
But that's not actually

00:26:25.709 --> 00:26:27.085
how most software gets written.

00:26:27.085 --> 00:26:29.462
Actually, I think maybe the
killer enterprise feature

00:26:29.462 --> 00:26:30.630
is refactors, right?

00:26:30.630 --> 00:26:33.633
It's like rewriting your
COBOL app or changing-

00:26:33.633 --> 00:26:37.804
When Facebook did hip
hop to do static PHP.

00:26:37.804 --> 00:26:38.638
If you think about it,

00:26:38.638 --> 00:26:41.725
the amount of deep, sophisticated thought

00:26:41.725 --> 00:26:43.768
that is required to accomplish a refactor

00:26:43.768 --> 00:26:44.894
is actually not that high.

00:26:44.894 --> 00:26:46.313
There's a lot of mechanical work

00:26:46.313 --> 00:26:48.898
that's just the sheer
volume of it that's hard,

00:26:48.898 --> 00:26:52.277
That's an AI-shaped problem for sure.

00:26:52.277 --> 00:26:55.447
I think we're going to see
a lot more productivity

00:26:55.447 --> 00:26:58.533
on all sorts of AI tasks as a result.

00:26:58.533 --> 00:27:01.995
Now, we are in a world where
you said a second thing,

00:27:01.995 --> 00:27:03.413
which is maybe you need

00:27:03.413 --> 00:27:04.706
to narrow down more.

00:27:04.706 --> 00:27:06.416
I think that the way these models work

00:27:06.416 --> 00:27:07.917
is you actually do want one model

00:27:07.917 --> 00:27:09.836
that knows more and more things,

00:27:09.836 --> 00:27:12.881
and you want it to have
some personalization to you,

00:27:12.881 --> 00:27:14.424
but the fact that is

00:27:14.424 --> 00:27:15.967
they have this one base model

00:27:15.967 --> 00:27:17.552
that kind of knows everything is actually

00:27:17.552 --> 00:27:18.720
a very useful starting point.

00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:20.555
So I do think that you're
going to see a world

00:27:20.555 --> 00:27:22.932
where we'll have more and
more capable base models

00:27:22.932 --> 00:27:24.476
and figuring out how you really connect it

00:27:24.476 --> 00:27:25.310
to all of your organization's code,

00:27:25.310 --> 00:27:26.144
and context, and history.

00:27:28.396 --> 00:27:32.317
How does OpenAI decide
what products to do?

00:27:32.317 --> 00:27:35.779
I'm just curious how you think about

00:27:35.779 --> 00:27:38.698
when to develop specific
products or when you think,

00:27:38.698 --> 00:27:40.450
"Oh, you contributed do that with ChatGPT,

00:27:40.450 --> 00:27:41.534
and that's good enough."

00:27:41.534 --> 00:27:43.495
Yeah. It's is a really
tough question, right?

00:27:43.495 --> 00:27:44.746
It's something we really struggle with,

00:27:44.746 --> 00:27:45.580
and I think that over time

00:27:46.539 --> 00:27:49.209
when we first launched ChatGPT,

00:27:49.209 --> 00:27:52.253
we're left with this, "Well,
we're an enterprise business,

00:27:52.253 --> 00:27:53.797
and we're a consumer business."

00:27:53.797 --> 00:27:56.508
And seems terrifying, as a startup.

00:27:56.508 --> 00:28:00.762
I remember talking to one of
my board members who said,

00:28:00.762 --> 00:28:01.846
"It just feels like what you have

00:28:01.846 --> 00:28:03.640
is an unfocused strategy at first,

00:28:03.640 --> 00:28:06.476
because you're just doing
all these different things."

00:28:06.476 --> 00:28:07.769
But if you think about it,

00:28:07.769 --> 00:28:09.688
maybe an analogies to
a company like Disney

00:28:09.688 --> 00:28:13.441
where you make one core asset
like Little Mermaid, right?

00:28:13.441 --> 00:28:15.694
Then, you productize it

00:28:15.694 --> 00:28:16.986
in all these different ways.

00:28:16.986 --> 00:28:18.363
You think about Little Mermaid the ride,

00:28:18.363 --> 00:28:21.116
the lunchbox, the T-shirt.

00:28:21.116 --> 00:28:22.575
I think that we have some element of that.

00:28:22.575 --> 00:28:23.910
We have a core model,

00:28:23.910 --> 00:28:24.828
and then we have a question of,

00:28:24.828 --> 00:28:26.037
"Well, what are the applications

00:28:26.037 --> 00:28:29.249
that this can add a lot of
value to quickly, right?

00:28:29.249 --> 00:28:31.167
With like a small amount
of additional work.

00:28:31.167 --> 00:28:34.629
So I think the question of
what areas to go into are

00:28:34.629 --> 00:28:36.881
how far does it take us
off the general path,

00:28:37.716 --> 00:28:41.010
the return on how important
is this domain area,

00:28:41.010 --> 00:28:43.555
especially for achieving the bigger goal,

00:28:43.555 --> 00:28:45.348
how much synergy is there across it

00:28:45.348 --> 00:28:46.975
with respect to other things we work on?

00:28:46.975 --> 00:28:49.352
So coding is one where
there's very clear synergy,

00:28:49.352 --> 00:28:50.603
very clear ROI.

00:28:50.603 --> 00:28:52.439
Because if we can speed ourselves up,

00:28:52.439 --> 00:28:55.150
that's something that
accelerates everything.

00:28:55.150 --> 00:28:58.236
How is being from North
Dakota, how has it shaped you?

00:29:00.238 --> 00:29:01.781
Look, North Dakota was an
amazing place to grow up.

00:29:01.781 --> 00:29:03.450
Was it actually? Come on.

00:29:04.492 --> 00:29:05.326
I've been there.

00:29:06.536 --> 00:29:07.495
And it was great.

00:29:08.538 --> 00:29:09.622
Look, it was incredibly safe.

00:29:09.622 --> 00:29:11.624
Our doors didn't even have working locks.

00:29:11.624 --> 00:29:13.668
It was that kind of place.

00:29:13.668 --> 00:29:15.211
I had a lot of freedom academically.

00:29:15.211 --> 00:29:17.797
Sixth grade, my dad
taught me some algebra.

00:29:17.797 --> 00:29:19.257
Seventh grade was the first time

00:29:19.257 --> 00:29:20.759
they split you into advanced math,

00:29:20.759 --> 00:29:22.844
so I was going to be taking pre-algebra.

00:29:22.844 --> 00:29:25.722
My mom took me to go see the teacher,

00:29:25.722 --> 00:29:27.348
and we asked, "Can you skip?"

00:29:27.348 --> 00:29:29.601
The teacher looked at
us very condescendingly

00:29:29.601 --> 00:29:31.770
and said, "Every parent believes

00:29:31.770 --> 00:29:33.855
that their child is special.

00:29:33.855 --> 00:29:35.231
I can guarantee your son will be

00:29:35.231 --> 00:29:36.733
plenty challenged in my class."

00:29:36.733 --> 00:29:39.152
And so, after a month of
me sitting in the back

00:29:39.152 --> 00:29:40.653
just playing games on my calculator

00:29:40.653 --> 00:29:43.198
and you know, she'd call me
randomly to try to trip me up,

00:29:43.198 --> 00:29:46.326
and I just look at the
board and be like, "2x."

00:29:46.326 --> 00:29:47.952
She said, "Okay, fair enough.

00:29:47.952 --> 00:29:50.497
Your son has nothing to
learn in this class."

00:29:50.497 --> 00:29:52.916
So, they moved me into
eighth grade algebra.

00:29:52.916 --> 00:29:54.709
But then eighth grade rolled around

00:29:54.709 --> 00:29:57.420
and I had no more math
left in my middle school.

00:29:57.420 --> 00:29:58.713
That's right you went to college, right?

00:29:58.713 --> 00:30:00.590
Well, so I did, in high school

00:30:00.590 --> 00:30:02.342
start going to University of North Dakota,

00:30:02.342 --> 00:30:04.135
and took a bunch of classes there.

00:30:04.135 --> 00:30:06.721
But also, I was connected
to a lot of people

00:30:06.721 --> 00:30:09.182
who were the top math kids in the country

00:30:09.182 --> 00:30:11.476
through things like math camp
and the math competitions.

00:30:11.476 --> 00:30:12.685
So, you're saying the social scene

00:30:12.685 --> 00:30:15.104
was not too distracting in North Dakota?

00:30:15.104 --> 00:30:16.564
Not too distracting,

00:30:16.564 --> 00:30:19.067
but it was definitely fun.

00:30:19.067 --> 00:30:20.318
Last question, do you remember

00:30:20.318 --> 00:30:25.114
we were going to Camp YC in 2017

00:30:25.114 --> 00:30:29.828
and I asked you how far AGI was away

00:30:29.828 --> 00:30:31.496
and you said two or three years.

00:30:32.497 --> 00:30:33.331
Did I say that?

00:30:33.331 --> 00:30:34.457
You did.

00:30:34.457 --> 00:30:35.750
I don't see the recording.

00:30:35.750 --> 00:30:39.504
Well, I'm just trying to think

00:30:39.504 --> 00:30:40.797
was I right, were you right?

00:30:40.797 --> 00:30:41.714
How should we grade that?

00:30:41.714 --> 00:30:43.925
Because we didn't get AGI,

00:30:43.925 --> 00:30:46.636
but we didn't not get AGI either.

00:30:46.636 --> 00:30:49.055
I'm curious if you have any reflections

00:30:49.055 --> 00:30:52.141
from your own AGI prediction journey.

00:30:53.142 --> 00:30:55.562
I think that we are...

00:30:55.562 --> 00:30:57.856
I will say I think that AI is surprising.

00:30:57.856 --> 00:31:01.025
I think that that is like the
single most consistent theme,

00:31:01.025 --> 00:31:02.861
is that the thing we were picturing,

00:31:02.861 --> 00:31:03.987
we got something different,

00:31:03.987 --> 00:31:06.531
but we got something better, more magical.

00:31:06.531 --> 00:31:07.991
Something that is more helpful.

00:31:07.991 --> 00:31:10.618
And so, I'm actually
quite happy with that.

00:31:10.618 --> 00:31:12.120
Now, predicting where you go,

00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:14.747
it's again, it's really hard
to manage the outputs here.

00:31:14.747 --> 00:31:17.750
One goal of OpenAI that we
have successfully achieved,

00:31:17.750 --> 00:31:21.170
is every year to have at least one result

00:31:21.170 --> 00:31:23.339
that just feels like
a step function better

00:31:23.339 --> 00:31:24.966
than anything before.

00:31:24.966 --> 00:31:26.301
You know when you see it kind of.

00:31:26.301 --> 00:31:29.554
You just have one really awesome
AI feeling thing each year.

00:31:29.554 --> 00:31:30.388
That kind of thing.

00:31:30.388 --> 00:31:31.681
I like that.
Yeah.

00:31:31.681 --> 00:31:32.974
That's a good way to tie it back,

00:31:32.974 --> 00:31:36.060
which is, you know, the way
we grade that prediction

00:31:36.060 --> 00:31:38.771
is that you've stopped setting
metrics based on outputs.

00:31:38.771 --> 00:31:40.148
Yeah, exactly, exactly.
Yeah. Yeah.

00:31:40.148 --> 00:31:42.483
Yes. But it does feel
we're getting really close

00:31:42.483 --> 00:31:44.360
to something pretty magical.

00:31:44.360 --> 00:31:46.571
I agree. Thank you.
Thank you.

